{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793872322,"nonce":"0k+MUfvd+dUsO3nSjvgkJ80fNfc=","title":"Browser Calendar stays consumed or cancelled across continuous wheel swipes","message":"Owner clarified that continuous Calendar swipes fail in the browser while native works. This is distinct from the original two-calendar isolation report d358a568bb2a99a202287bf3ad1c8aaaece6e4126d0e0839436b58090361c01d.\n\nReproduced in isolated Chrome worker-WebGPU against runtime c1bb5ce44069087752a7711e1111bbab9b6d86a29773df54dde6a2dcf3fa7abc. Four gradual same-direction swipes without clicks or idle gaps advanced only to January instead of March. Cancelling a partial swipe by moving outside the calendar (remaining within the canvas), then immediately returning and swiping again, left November displayed instead of December.\n\nNative baseline: all 13 cn_calendar headless Metal tests pass, including two-calendar ownership/cancellation. The browser wheel recognizer in crates/blinc_app/src/web_wheel.rs infers touch boundaries from DOM wheel events. It compares renewed acceleration against only the previous delta and requires one sample to double. Smooth ramps never satisfy that, so later finger strokes keep the consumed/cancelled gesture identity. A deterministic per-frame-drain regression recognizes only one start in four strokes before the fix.\n\nIn progress: compare renewed acceleration to the minimum reached by the decaying tail, retain pause/reversal and momentum-noise guards, and verify repeated swipes plus cancellation/re-entry in actual Chrome at DPR1/2. Native calendar handlers need no changes. Regression test web/components/tests/calendar_browser.py dispatches through real DOM/host/worker listeners and reads the rendered month, with timing generated inside the browser so automation round trips cannot introduce a false idle reset.\n\nRelease coordinator: 76a15bcfbb889c0af7e9ee97bf54cc40488a573eeaa0f92d136b0a6b3c1a32b6. Include this fix in 0.6.1 with the new blinc crate. No claim of new native/physical-Magic-Mouse behaviour or publication.","files":null}]}